"} "Baseball"] "\n" [:option {:value "BKB"} "Basketball"] "\n" [:option {:value "BOX"} "Boxing"] "\n" [:option {:value "CTR"} "Cycling Track"] "\n" [:option {:value "DIV"} "Diving"] "\n" [:option {:value "FEN"} "Fencing"] "\n" [:option {:value "FBL"} "Football"] "\n" [:option {:value "GLF"} "Golf"] "\n" [:option {:value "LAX"} "Lacrosse"] "\n" [:option {:value "ROQ"} "Roque"] "\n" [:option {:value "ROW"} "Rowing"] "\n" [:option {:value "SWM"} "Swimming"] "\n" [:option {:value "TEN"} "Tennis"] "\n" [:option {:value "TOW"} "Tug-Of-War"] "\n" [:option {:value "WPO"} "Water Polo"] "\n" [:option {:value "WLF"} "Weightlifting"] "\n" [:option {:value "WRE"} "Wrestling"]]] [:script {} "$('#sports_in_same_games_select').change(function(){location.href='/editions/3/sports/'+$('#sports_in_same_games_select').val()})"] [:form {:id "other_games_form", :class "form-inline", :style "display: inline", :method "get"} [:select {:name "year", :id "sport_in_other_games_select", :class "form-control"} [:option {:value ""} "Archery in other Games"] "\n" [:option {:value "2"} "1900 Summer Olympics"] "\n" [:option {:value "5"} "1908 Summer Olympics"] "\n" [:option {:value "7"} "1920 Summer Olympics"] "\n" [:option {:value "18"} "1972 Summer Olympics"] "\n" [:option {:value "19"} "1976 Summer Olympics"] "\n" [:option {:value "20"} "1980 Summer Olympics"] "\n" [:option {:value "21"} "1984 Summer Olympics"] "\n" [:option {:value "22"} "1988 Summer Olympics"] "\n" [:option {:value "23"} "1992 Summer Olympics"] "\n" [:option {:value "24"} "1996 Summer Olympics"] "\n" [:option {:value "25"} "2000 Summer Olympics"] "\n" [:option {:value "26"} "2004 Summer Olympics"] "\n" [:option {:value "53"} "2008 Summer Olympics"] "\n" [:option {:value "65"} "2010 Summer Youth Olympics"] "\n" [:option {:value "54"} "2012 Summer Olympics"] "\n" [:option {:value "67"} "2014 Summer Youth Olympics"] "\n" [:option {:value "59"} "2016 Summer Olympics"] "\n" [:option {:value "69"} "2018 Summer Youth Olympics"] "\n" [:option {:value "61"} "2020 Summer Olympics"] "\n" [:option {:value "63"} "2024 Summer Olympics"]]] [:script {} "$('#sport_in_other_games_select').change(function(){location.href='/editions/' + $('#sport_in_other_games_select').val() + '/sports/ARC'})"] "\n" [:h1 {} "Archery at the 1904 Summer Olympics"] "\n" [:table {:class "biodata"} "\n" [:tbody {} [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} "Dates"] "\n" [:td {} "12 August – 21 September 1904"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} "Medal Events"] "\n" [:td {} "6"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} "Non-medal Events"] "\n" [:td {} "3"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} "Venues"] "\n" [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/210"} "Francis Field, Washington University, St. Louis"]] "\n"] "\n"]] "\n" [:div {:class "description"} "\n" [:p {} "The archery events of the 1904 Olympics were contested at the World’s Fairgrounds, on 19-21 September 1904.  These events were actually the United States National Championship, whose formal name was the 26th Grand Annual Target Meeting of the National Archery Association (NAA) of the USA.  Fortunately, due to the efforts of Mr. Robert Rhode, historian of the National Archery Association, there are available to us absolutely complete results of the archery events of the 1904 Olympics in four different forms.  One is a printout from the National Archery Association.  A second is the actual handwritten scores from the Secretary of the National Archery Association in that year, Lewis Maxson.  The third is the results which were published in the British archery magazine, " [:em {} "The Archer’s Register"] ", while the fourth is the results as published in Spalding’s " [:em {} "Official Athletic Almanac"] "."] "\n\n" [:p {} "However, we consider the archery events of 1904 to be of marginal Olympic caliber.  These were essentially United States’ National Championships.  No professionals competed and, unfortunately, no foreign archers competed either.  There were some foreign entrants from the Philippine islands and the Lanao Moro tribe.  However, the Filipino archers did not show or compete."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The rules of the archery events were listed in the programme as stating that, “Medals of the NAA open only to competitions of members.  Competitions for medals offered by universal exposition open to all archers.”  Thus, it would appear that the events were open to all archers.  Still, with no foreign athletes competing, it is really difficult to call these events much more than the United States’ National Championships."] "\n\n" [:p {} "There are also some problems with several of the events that were contested.  Men competed in the " [:a {:href "/results/40507"} "Double York round"] " and " [:a {:href "/results/40511"} "Double American round"] ".  The women competed in the " [:a {:href "/results/40516"} "Double National round"] " and " [:a {:href "/results/40519"} "Double Columbia round"] ".  There was also a " [:a {:href "/results/40515"} "gentlemen’s team round"] " which was contested.  A " [:a {:href "/results/41463"} "ladies’ team round"] " was scheduled, and most sources mention it was not held, as only one team appeared, that from Cincinnati.  It is often listed in several Olympic reference books that a women’s team round did occur and that medals were given out, but this is not listed in most of the final results. However, some more recent sources give results for this event, certainly implying that the archers did at least shoot a round to earn the medal."] "\n\n" [:p {} [:em {} "The Archer’s Register"] " also listed medals given for the “Olympic range medals” for each of the individual events, and also for flight shooting for men and women.  The Olympic range medals basically were for the highest score at each separate distance of the various individual rounds.  Although a medal was given out for these, they are certainly of marginal Olympic significance and, in effect, similar to giving out medals in the modern pentathlon for the best score in each of the events.  Flight shooting is more difficult to dismiss.  The " [:a {:href "/results/925613"} "men’s flight shooting"] " was won by " [:a {:href "/athletes/2238"} "Louis Maxson"] ", with a distance of 259 yards, while the " [:a {:href "/results/925614"} "women’s flight shooting"] " was won by " [:a {:href "/athletes/2220"} "Mabel Taylor"] ", with a distance of 219 yards.  No other results are given.  Possibly, these two events carried as much importance as the individual rounds that were contested, but it is also possible that they were considered only a championship of the NAA, which is more likely.  Given that, we have not included them as Olympic events."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The archery events were dominated by " [:a {:href "/athletes/2225"} "Phil Bryant"] " of Boston, who won both of the men’s individual rounds and helped his Boston team to a bronze medal in the team round.  The men’s team round saw Reverend " [:a {:href "/athletes/2246"} "G. C. Spencer"] " win a gold medal with the Potomac Archers of Washington.  Spencer turned 64 years of age on the day prior to winning his gold medal, which, as of 2024, makes him the second oldest American Olympic gold medalist ever, behind " [:a {:href "/athletes/102561"} "Charles Jacobus"] " (who was 97 days older than Spencer, and also won his gold at St. Louis, in the sport of " [:a {:href "/results/30600"} "roque"] "). Finishing second in the team round event was a team of Cincinnati archers who included an even older marksman, " [:a {:href "/athletes/2230"} "Samuel Duvall"] ", who was 68 years of age at the 1904 Olympics and is the oldest American Olympic medalist as of 2024 (if one excludes " [:a {:href "/athletes/921564"} "Frederick MacMonnies"] ", who won the silver at the " [:a {:href "/results/920040"} "art competitions in 1932"] ", and was over 100 days older than Duvall)."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The archery events for women were dominated by " [:a {:href "/athletes/2209"} "Lida Howell"] ", who was 17 times American ladies archery champion, had no peer at all in that era, and is considered one of the greatest women archers of all time in the United States."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Placings in the individual events were determined as follows: one point was awarded for the high score at each distance, one point for the high number of hits at each distance, two points were for the high total score, and two points for the high total number of hits, making ten points possible in all.  Ties were broken by total score.  Any remaining ties were broken by total number of hits."] "\n\n"] "\n\n" [:h2 {} "Events"] "\n" [:table {:class "table table-striped"} "\n" [:thead {} "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} "Event"] "\n" [:th {} "Status"] "\n" [:th {} "Date"] "\n" [:th {} "Participants"] "\n" [:th {} "NOCs"] "\n"] "\n"] "\n" [:tbody {} [:tr {} "\n" [:td {} [:a {:href "/results/40511"} "Double American Round, Men"]] "\n" [:td {} "Olympic"] "\n" [:td {} "19 September 1904"] "\n" [:td {} "22"] "\n" [:td {} "1"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:td {} [:a {:href "/results/40507"} "Double York Round, Men"]] "\n" [:td {} "Olympic"] "\n" [:td {} "20 September 1904"] "\n" [:td {} "16"] "\n" [:td {} "1"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:td {} [:a {:href "/results/40515"} "Team Round, Men"]] "\n" [:td {} "Olympic"] "\n" [:td {} "21 September 1904"] "\n" [:td {} "16"] "\n" [:td {} "1"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:td {} [:a {:href "/results/40519"} "Double Columbia Round, Women"]] "\n" [:td {} "Olympic"] "\n" [:td {} "19 September 1904"] "\n" [:td {} "6"] "\n" [:td {} "1"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:td {} [:a {:href "/results/40516"} "Double National Round, Women"]] "\n" [:td {} "Olympic"] "\n" [:td {} "20 September 1904"] "\n" [:td {} "6"] "\n" [:td {} "1"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:td {} [:a {:href "/results/41463"} "Team Round, Women"]] "\n" [:td {} "Olympic"] "\n" [:td {} "21 September 1904"] "\n" [:td {} "4"] "\n" [:td {} "1"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:td {}] "\n" [:td {}] "\n" [:td {}] "\n" [:td {:class "border-top"} "\n29 (23/6)\n"] "\n" [:td {:class "bord